{"id":"h188","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"claim","question":"Is the following claim true? Johannes Kepler published Astronomia Nova in 1609.","truth":"yes","model_answer":"yes","correct":true,"source":"author-written for this project; verified by the author","claims":[{"text":"Kepler's Astronomia Nova was published in 1609.","confidence":0.97,"lean":1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Astronomia Nova presented Kepler's first two laws of planetary motion, based on analysis of Mars's orbit.","confidence":0.95,"lean":0.2,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The title page and standard references give 1609 as the publication year.","confidence":0.93,"lean":1,"theta":1.4608,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Histories of astronomy widely cite it as Kepler's 1609 work.","confidence":0.9,"lean":1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The book was printed in Heidelberg, not Prague.","confidence":0.8,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.2566,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The printing location does not make the claim false, since the claim mentions no place.","confidence":0.9,"lean":1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Kepler's third law appeared later in Harmonices Mundi (1619).","confidence":0.95,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Confusion over the dates of Kepler's works could cast doubt on the 1609 date, but it affects only content, not this book's date.","confidence":0.15,"lean":-0.3,"theta":0.2356,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The 1609 publication date is well documented and matches the claim.","confidence":0.95,"lean":1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.3,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":2,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":3,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":5,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":7,"J":0.2,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":8,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":6,"J":0.3,"relation":"supports"},{"i":2,"j":3,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":2,"j":5,"J":0.3,"relation":"supports"},{"i":2,"j":8,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i"